% lib_draft/deontic_history.le -- a first cut of lib/deontic_history.le, the
% library of the design notes l4.md section 4.1 (the timeless reading of L4's
% contracts). Draft: it lives here until step 3 moves it to LE2's lib/.
%
% A contract is a chain of obligations. The program says, for each kind of
% obligation, when one starts, its deadline (if it has one), and which events
% meet it. This library says, over a finished history, how each obligation
% ends: met on a day, or missed on a day, or still pending.
%
% An obligation is written as a sentence of the program's own
% (`the borrower pays the instalment on a debt of 27077.49`), so that a
% contract with parameters -- an amount owed, a party -- needs no numbering.
%
% L4's evaluator notices a missed deadline only when something next happens
% (a later event, or the history saying time has passed). That event starts
% the next obligation and does not itself count towards it. The library reads
% deadlines the same way.
%
% What the program supplies:
%     the obligation that *a sentence* starts on *a date*.
%     the obligation that *a sentence* has deadline *a date*.       (none: no deadline)
%     the obligation that *a sentence* is met on *a date*.
%     something happens on *a date*.
%     an obligation can start on *a date*.         (every date an obligation
%        may start on: the contract's start and the history's dates. LE's loop
%        check needs a recursive call's date bound before the call; see
%        the design notes l4.md section 7.)

the templates are:
    the obligation that *a sentence* starts on *a date*.
    the obligation that *a sentence* has deadline *a date*.
    the obligation that *a sentence* is met on *a date*.
    something happens on *a date*.
    an obligation can start on *a date*.
    the obligation that *a sentence* ends on *a date*.
    the obligation that *a sentence* is missed on *a date*.
    the obligation that *a sentence* is pending since *a date*.
    the deadline of the obligation that *a sentence* has passed on *a date*.

the knowledge base deontic history includes:

the deadline of the obligation that a sentence has passed on a day if
    something happens on the day
    and an obligation can start on a start
    and the start is before the day
    and the obligation that the sentence starts on the start
    and the obligation that the sentence has deadline a deadline
    and the day is after the deadline.

the obligation that a sentence ends on a day if
    the obligation that the sentence is met on the day
    and it is not the case that
        the deadline of the obligation that the sentence has passed on an earlier day
        and the earlier day is before the day
    and it is not the case that
        the obligation that the sentence is met on an other day
        and the other day is before the day.

the obligation that a sentence is missed on a day if
    the deadline of the obligation that the sentence has passed on the day
    and it is not the case that
        the obligation that the sentence ends on a paid day
    and it is not the case that
        the deadline of the obligation that the sentence has passed on an earlier day
        and the earlier day is before the day.

the obligation that a sentence is pending since a date if
    an obligation can start on the date
    and the obligation that the sentence starts on the date
    and it is not the case that
        the obligation that the sentence ends on a day
    and it is not the case that
        the obligation that the sentence is missed on a day.
